# Existence of a separating transcendental over a perfect field

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.exists_separating_transcendental_of_perfectField`
(`a9b8b10c-f084-5a40-8677-5f0e6588bb12`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `792d0909-c16c-55f0-97a0-e8101b570ba7`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $K$ perfect and $F$ a $K$-algebra, and let $x \in F$ be
transcendental over $K$ (i.e. $x$ is not a root of any nonzero polynomial over $K$) and such that
$F$ is finite-dimensional as a module over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $\{x\}$ inside
$F$. Then there exists $t \in F$ with three properties: $t$ is transcendental over $K$; $F$ is
finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(t)$ generated by $\{t\}$; and the extension
$F/K(t)$ is separable, in the sense of the `Algebra.IsSeparable` predicate, namely every element of
$F$ has separable minimal polynomial over $K(t)$. Thus an algebraic function field of one variable
over a perfect field, presented by one transcendental generator $x$ with $[F:K(x)] < \infty$, admits
a separating transcendental element.

This is F. K. Schmidt's theorem that a one-variable algebraic function field over a perfect constant
field is separably generated; no hypothesis on the exact constant field of $F$ beyond perfectness of
$K$ is made. It underpins the curve-theoretic part of the development, being used for instance in
the results on places, regular prolongations and the degree-zero Picard group of a curve.

